ent-SpawnMobGubbuck = gubbuck spawner
    .desc = Spawns a male gutlunch when the map starts.
ent-SpawnMobGuthen = guthen spawner
    .desc = Spawns a female gutlunch when the map starts.
ent-BaseMobGutlunch = gutlunch
    .desc = A placid scavenger with a nutrient sac. It digests raw meat into rich milk.
ent-MobGubbuck = gubbuck
    .desc = A small, colourful male gutlunch. It eats raw meat and produces nutritious milk. A well-fed gubbuck can mate with a guthen.
ent-MobGuthen = guthen
    .desc = A female gutlunch. It eats raw meat and produces nutritious milk. With a full sac and a well-fed male nearby, it can bear a single young.
gutlunch-examine-milk = Nutrient sac: [color=yellow]{ $amount }/{ $capacity }[/color].
gutlunch-examine-pregnant = She is carrying a young.

ash-walker-recipe-book-husbandry =
    { "[" }bold]Gutlunch husbandry[/bold]
    Keep a guthen and a smaller, russet gubbuck in a basalt pen with a gate. They cannot open gates themselves. Ash storms and lava do not harm them.
    Feed them raw meat from butchered prey. Offer a piece by hand or leave it on the pen floor: they seek accessible food nearby. Whole corpses and cooked food are unsuitable.
    Each piece is eaten whole. More remaining raw animal protein means more milk. Slicing adds no yield. A full sac turns pale and holds 50 units. Avoid wasting a large piece on a nearly full sac.
    Offer an open bowl or bucket and hold it steady for five seconds. Both sexes can be milked. Three parts cream and two parts saline make a nourishing drink that also restores fluids and blood. No meat means no new milk.
    For offspring, leave the female's sac full and the male's at least half full. Keep them within arm's reach. Mating consumes the female's entire sac and 25 units from the male.
    Gestation takes two minutes and produces one young. It matures in three more minutes and cannot breed before then. A herd is limited to six animals within 32 paces; unborn young also occupy a place.
    Gutlunches have no value to the obelisk. Protect the breeding pair: butchering their young yields less meat than breeding consumed.
